+1. **High — “imported last” does not establish the promised cascade, and the proposed tests would miss that.** `.tone-positive:hover` has the same specificity as `.fb-copy:hover`, so order works there, but it loses to `.fb-copy[aria-disabled="true"]:hover` and `.fb-send:not(:disabled):hover`. The latter could make a negative-toned `.fb-send` turn green on hover if its existing paint rules remain. The plan explicitly removes only the four reply-thread rules, leaving the fate of `.fb-send` paint ambiguous. Current buttons may happen to look right, but the shared design-system contract is not true. The test plan checks declarations and import order rather than the computed winner; a higher-specificity selector can regress the result while those checks remain green. Preserve/adapt the existing specificity and paint-rule inventory tests, or add browser computed-style coverage for rest, hover, disabled, `aria-disabled`, and `data-working` in both themes. Evidence: [plan:28](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/docs/plans/261020f-feedback-buttons-coloured-positive-and-negative.md:28>), [plan:44](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/docs/plans/261020f-feedback-buttons-coloured-positive-and-negative.md:44>), [feedback.css:729](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/src/web/styles/feedback.css:729>), [feedback.css:733](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/src/web/styles/feedback.css:733>), [feedback.css:783](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/src/web/styles/feedback.css:783>), [current specificity test:116](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/tests/feedback-dialog-styles.test.ts:116>).
+
+2. **Medium — the plan omits a literal Close button from “throughout the dialog.”** The header cross is a button with `aria-label="Close"`, and its feedback-specific colour is currently grey/orange. Applying `tone-negative` to this particular `.fb-close` would not require changing the shared `.close-x` component, so the stated reason for excluding it does not follow. Either include it or record explicit confirmation that “Close” means only the footer text button. Evidence: [plan:35](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/docs/plans/261020f-feedback-buttons-coloured-positive-and-negative.md:35>), [plan:46](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/docs/plans/261020f-feedback-buttons-coloured-positive-and-negative.md:46>), [FeedbackDialog.tsx:1252](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/src/web/FeedbackDialog.tsx:1252>), [feedback.css:499](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/src/web/styles/feedback.css:499>).
+
+3. **Medium — “disabled dimming stays the family’s own” is false for the negative `.fb-copy` buttons.** Addendum Cancel and Defer use native `disabled`, but `.fb-copy` only dims `[aria-disabled="true"]`; the global disabled rule changes only the cursor. Consequently, Cancel remains full-strength red while Addendum is sending, and Defer remains full-strength when unavailable because Reply is sending. The plan needs to decide and test these states; if a working Defer should remain strong while an unavailable Defer dims, it will also need a working-state distinction. Evidence: [plan:33](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/docs/plans/261020f-feedback-buttons-coloured-positive-and-negative.md:33>), [FeedbackAddendum.tsx:255](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/src/web/FeedbackAddendum.tsx:255>), [FeedbackEarlier.tsx:1403](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/src/web/FeedbackEarlier.tsx:1403>), [feedback.css:730](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/src/web/styles/feedback.css:730>), [tailwind.css:379](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/src/web/tailwind.css:379>).
+
+4. **Medium — the required design-system rule is deferred rather than delivered, and its semantics need narrowing.** `/design` plus a stylesheet comment demonstrates a component, but the authoritative controls document would still omit the rule. The plan should include the approved `controls.md` wording before completion, not leave it as a later feedback question. That wording should say this is a labelled send/back-out shortcut, not a general “red means negative/destructive” rule: Cancel and Close preserve the Write draft, while existing docs describe `--danger` as error text and `--destructive` as the destructive fill. Evidence: [plan:48](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/docs/plans/261020f-feedback-buttons-coloured-positive-and-negative.md:48>), [plan:53](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/docs/plans/261020f-feedback-buttons-coloured-positive-and-negative.md:53>), [controls.md:24](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/docs/project/controls.md:24>), [FeedbackDialog.tsx:643](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/src/web/FeedbackDialog.tsx:643>), [design-css-overview.md:100](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/docs/project/design-css-overview.md:100>).
+
+The red/green pair is not itself an accessibility blocker: both tokens meet the planned 4.5:1 contrast checks in both themes, and visible action labels mean colour is supplementary rather than the sole carrier. Red–green colour-blind users may lose the shortcut, but not the meaning. Evidence: [appearance-palette.test.ts:87](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/tests/appearance-palette.test.ts:87>), [colour-scales.md:413](</var/tmp/spideryarn-worktrees/fbas0et6-feedback-button-colours/docs/project/colour-scales.md:413>).
+
+**Verdict: REWORK**
